{"qid":"Q7135130","names":["Paramara Dynasty"],"title":"Paramara Dynasty","endonym":null,"capsule":"The Paramara dynasty ruled the Malwa plateau of central India (roughly modern Madhya Pradesh) from their capital at Dhara (modern Dhar), having become an independent power under Siyaka II, who sacked the Rashtrakuta capital Manyakheta around 972. His successor Munja (r. c. 972-990) campaigned aggressively into the Deccan but was defeated and killed by the Western Chalukya king Tailapa II. The dynasty reached its zenith under Bhoja (r. c. 1010-1055), a celebrated scholar-king who authored treatises on poetics, architecture, and yoga, built the great Bhojeshvara temple at Bhojpur, and was renowned for his patronage of learning. After Bhoja, the Paramaras endured repeated invasions by the Chaulukyas, Chandelas, and later the Yadavas; their power steadily contracted through the twelfth and thirteenth centuries even as the line persisted in Malwa.","attestation":"Boundaries from Seshat Cliopatria (Bennett et al., Scientific Data 2025; CC BY 4.0), digitized from standard historical atlases at ~0.07° resolution. Paramara territory in Malwa is attested through inscriptions and copper-plate grants, but the dynasty's claimed dominions beyond the plateau core are difficult to verify and may reflect rhetorical rather than effective sovereignty.","attestationLinks":[{"label":"Cliopatria dataset (Seshat, CC BY 4.0)","url":"https://github.com/Seshat-Global-History-Databank/cliopatria"},{"label":"Bennett et al. 2025, Scientific Data","url":"https://www.nature.com/articles/s41597-025-04516-9"}],"citations":[],"britannica":null,"cowCode":null,"wikipedia":"Paramara dynasty","rulers":[{"qid":null,"name":"Upendra (Krishnaraja)","nativeName":"उपेन्द्र (कृष्णराज)","roman":"Upendra (Kṛṣṇarāja)","start":800,"end":818,"image":null,"imageUrl":null,"imageLicense":null},{"qid":null,"name":"Vairisimha I","nativeName":"वैरिसिंह I","roman":"Vairisimha I","start":818,"end":843,"image":null,"imageUrl"